  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "s1",
      "short": "One DB",
      "title": "One database, doing everything",
      "chapter": "problem",
      "body": "Every read and every write goes to a single database server. It’s 90% busy — and most of that is reads: product pages, profiles, feeds.\n\nIt’s also a single point of failure: if it dies, so does everything.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "Most apps read far more than they write — often 10 or 100 reads per write."
      },
      "changes": [],
      "metrics": {
        "dbLoad": "90%",
        "reads": "1×",
        "writes": "ok"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "short": "Copies",
      "title": "Keep copies: leader and followers",
      "chapter": "solution",
      "body": "Add two [[followers]] (replicas). The [[leader]] still takes every write, and streams each change to the followers through a replication log.\n\nEach follower replays the changes and ends up with the same data.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Also called primary/replica or master/slave. Built into PostgreSQL, MySQL, MongoDB and most others."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Two followers",
        "+ Replication stream from the leader"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "dbLoad": "90%",
        "reads": "1×",
        "lag": "0 s",
        "writes": "ok"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "short": "Read replicas",
      "title": "Send reads to the followers",
      "chapter": "solution",
      "body": "Now the app sends **reads** to the followers and only **writes** to the leader. Read capacity doubles — two machines now do nothing but reads — and the leader cools down to 30%.\n\nNeed more reads? Add another follower.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "This works brilliantly for read-heavy apps. It doesn’t help write-heavy ones — every write still goes through one leader."
      },
      "changes": [
        "~ Reads → followers",
        "~ Writes → leader only",
        "~ Leader CPU 90% → 30%"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "dbLoad": "30%",
        "reads": "2×",
        "lag": "0 s",
        "writes": "ok"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s4",
      "short": "Lag",
      "title": "Copies lag behind",
      "chapter": "problem",
      "body": "Replication is usually [[asynchronous]]: the leader confirms a write right away and ships it to followers a moment later.\n\nAlice renames herself, then reloads her profile. The read lands on a follower that hasn’t caught up — she sees her **old** name and thinks the save failed.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "warning",
        "text": "Lag is normally milliseconds, but under load it can grow to seconds or minutes."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Alice writes a new name",
        "- Follower still has the old one"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "dbLoad": "30%",
        "reads": "2×",
        "lag": "2 s",
        "writes": "ok"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "users": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "f1-2": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "ua": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "a1": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s5",
      "short": "Read your writes",
      "title": "Read your own writes",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "body": "A common fix: for a few seconds after someone writes, send *their* reads to the leader. Everyone else keeps using followers.\n\nThe other option is [[synchronous]] replication: the leader waits for a follower to confirm before saying “saved”. No lag — but slower writes, and they stall if that follower is down.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "For data you can’t afford to lose, a common setup is one synchronous follower (for safety) and the rest asynchronous (for speed). Many systems stay fully asynchronous and accept losing the last moments of writes in a crash."
      },
      "changes": [
        "~ Alice’s reads → leader for 5 s",
        "~ She sees her new name"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "dbLoad": "32%",
        "reads": "2×",
        "lag": "2 s",
        "writes": "ok"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "users": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "f1-2": {
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "ua": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "a1": {
          "emphasis": true
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s6",
      "short": "Leader dies",
      "title": "The leader dies",
      "chapter": "problem",
      "body": "The leader crashes. Reads keep working — the followers still have the data — but every write now fails. Nobody can sign up, post or pay.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "warning",
        "text": "Followers protect your reads. Writes need a new leader."
      },
      "changes": [
        "- Leader down",
        "- Writes failing"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "dbLoad": "0%",
        "reads": "2×",
        "writes": "failing"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "users": {
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "f2-2": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "ua": {
          "emphasis": true
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s7",
      "short": "Failover",
      "title": "Failover: promote a follower",
      "chapter": "solution",
      "body": "[[Failover]]: the most up-to-date follower is promoted to leader, the app points its writes there, and the other follower replicates from it. A fresh follower is added to restore the spare.\n\nWrites resume within seconds to a minute. Any writes the old leader hadn’t shipped yet may be lost — another reason to keep one follower synchronous.\n\nThe hard part is being *sure* the leader is dead. If it was only slow or cut off, you now have two leaders both accepting writes — [[split brain]]. Doing this safely takes a majority vote: see *Consensus & Leader Election*.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "Same idea as the standby load balancer: a spare that takes over. Managed databases automate it."
      },
      "changes": [
        "- Old leader removed",
        "~ Follower promoted to leader",
        "+ New follower"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "dbLoad": "30%",
        "reads": "2×",
        "lag": "0 s",
        "writes": "ok"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "f2-2": {
          "emphasis": true
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s8",
      "short": "Reports",
      "title": "Case: the 20-minute report",
      "chapter": "problem",
      "body": "The finance team runs a monthly report — a 20-minute query that scans years of orders. They point it at a follower, and that follower, which also serves the app’s reads, grinds to a halt and falls minutes behind.\n\nFix: a dedicated **analytics replica** that the app never reads from. Heavy queries can be as slow as they like there.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "At larger scale, copy data into a separate analytics warehouse (BigQuery, Snowflake, Redshift) built for huge scans."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Analytics replica, outside the app’s read pool",
        "~ Reports no longer slow the app"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "dbLoad": "30%",
        "reads": "2×",
        "lag": "0 s",
        "writes": "ok"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s9",
      "short": "Not a backup",
      "title": "Case: the migration with no WHERE",
      "chapter": "problem",
      "body": "A deploy runs a migration with a missing `WHERE`: `UPDATE users SET email = NULL`. The leader obeys — and replication faithfully copies the mistake to every follower within a second.\n\nReplicas protect you from a *machine* failing, not from a bad write. For that you need [[backups]]: a nightly snapshot plus the archived write log, which together can rebuild the database as it was at any chosen moment — [[point-in-time recovery]].",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "warning",
        "text": "A backup you have never restored is a hope, not a backup. Rehearse restores, and know how long one takes."
      },
      "changes": [
        "- Bad migration blanks a column",
        "- Every replica copies the damage",
        "+ Nightly snapshot + write-log archive",
        "~ Restore to 14:02, a minute before the mistake"
      ],
