    {
      "id": "s10",
      "short": "Styles",
      "title": "Three ways to replicate",
      "chapter": "insight",
      "body": "Everything so far was **single-leader**: one writer, many readers. It’s simple and it’s what most SQL databases do.\n\n**Multi-leader** puts a leader in each region so writes stay local — at the cost of conflicts when two regions edit the same row. **Leaderless** systems (Cassandra, Riak, ScyllaDB — the “Dynamo-style” databases) write to several copies at once and read from several — the quorums in the CAP lesson.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "Prefer single-leader until you have a concrete reason (global writes, extreme availability) to take on conflict handling."
      },
